A||||17||BOILERMAKER||V6 Constant Speed Hesitation||||5
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||10-05-1999||09:04 AM||||I have an 87 V6 GT engine 5spd that has hesitation whenever I get up to speed in town in 2nd,3rd, or 4th gear somewhere between 1000 and 2000 RPM's.  This usually happens whenever I have finished accelerating and am at a constant speed (zero acceleration, constant velocity).  It is almost as if I have taken my foot off of the gas pedal for a partial second and returned to the same position, but my foot never moves.<P>The engine is stock.  The acceleration that I have in the car is the best.  I have no problems with acceleration or shifting or anything like that.  The only problem is when I am at this constant speed.  I can't feel this hesitation much when I am on the interstate in 5th gear.<P>I have changed spark plugs, wires, TPS (well, this was for another problem), O2 sensor (again for another problem) and this hesitation never goes away.  Any suggestions???  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||10-05-1999||09:57 AM||||Might be the IAC valve, cleaning it could help, but replacement is best.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||10-05-1999||10:40 AM||||Another thing you probably want to check is how much gas you have left... Reason is because on our Fiero's when your low..almost empty we don't get a signal from the computer...and I found out the hard way when I tried to race a VW Jetta...I thought I still have gas a little above the empty line..My car was hesitating in every gear that I shift and died on me too...Then I talk to my friend Keith and he said if there was enough gas in the car which I thought there was...Anyway...he ask me to put a little more gas and after I did the car start to life...{=o)||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||10-05-1999||11:00 AM||||Gas level has no bearing on when the hesitation occurs.  This happens when the tank is full or half-tank.  I rarely let the tank get below 1/4 tank.  I will check the IAC tonight.  For some reason I believe I have checked the IAC valve before and cleaned it, but the hesitation is still there.<P>Any other suggestions??||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||10-05-1999||12:19 PM||||Try Un-hooking The EGR Valve And See If It Still Does It. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Howard||10-05-1999||12:38 PM||||What is a IAC, and where is it located?||||reg||7
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||10-05-1999||01:06 PM||||Boilermaker, my V6/Auto does the exact same thing between 1600 and 2000 RPM. I know that doesn't help you much but it would be interesting to hear if you can find anything. <P>I personally think it has something to do with the EGR...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||10-05-1999||03:22 PM||||<BR>For what it's worth, I also vote for the EGR...||||reg||4
Z||000008||Standard||10-06-1999||03:07 AM||||Just to add another side to this, my 2.5/auto does the same thing, usually when I'm cruising around 30 mph.  I'm gonna try replacing the TPS when I get some money. <BR>Later||||reg||11
Z||000009||Carrolles||10-06-1999||07:09 AM||||Could it be a worn timing chain?||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||10-06-1999||12:20 PM||||check the map sensor and everything connected to it. If the vacuum line to this device is blocked or leaking slightly you can get some pretty strange things going on.<P>Also check the map sensor for a small vent hole. if the vent is blocked it will thro off the sensor.<P>Replace the TPS if it even looks bad. The moving parts of the sensor simply wear out. If it's never been replaced before then replace it anyway. If it never been replaced I'd about guarantee its severly worn.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||10-06-1999||12:27 PM||||I'm debating on buying a new EGR valve tonight and installing it and seeing what that has to offer.  I had a bad one and then replaced it with a spare part and pretty much forgot about it until now.<P>The TPS is new as of about a year ago and the problems that were related to that went away.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||10-07-1999||01:51 AM||||Where is the map sensor on the 2.5?  My haynes manual mentions it but there are no pictures or replacement instructions.  I'm guessing it's that little box with the plug on the side of the air cleaner?<BR>Thanx||||reg||5
Z||000013||BOILERMAKER||10-07-1999||08:38 AM||||Well, I changed my EGR valve last night.  I must admit that I needed a new one, but the old valve wasn't that bad.  It still worked, but the gasket was terrible.  So, I changed it anyway and put the old valve in the cabinet for safe keeping.<P>I drove it in today and I must admit that it did help a little.  It's kina like a hangover.  The edge is gone, but the headache is still there.  I guess I have check the MAP next, huh?||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||10-07-1999||09:33 AM||||MAP sensor on a 2.5 hangs off the air cleaner can.||||reg||1
Z||000015||ka4nkf||10-07-1999||12:49 PM||||Boilermaker Un-Hook The EGR Valve And Drive It. If It Dosent Miss,Then I Would Say That The EGR Vac. Control Switch Is Not Working Right. This Is The Switch That Is Located By The Thermostat Housing. It Has Two Vac. Lines Running To It. Also One Line Going To The Air Cleaner. It Is Expensive ,About $98.00. Mine Went Bad And I Just Un-Hooked The EGR Vale And Let It Go. It Runs Much Better And Has More Power.||||reg||1
Z||000016||mwbackus||10-07-1999||02:23 PM||||The Idle Air Control is responsible for obtaining a smooth idle.  This is also the case during cruise (ie.. steady throtle).  In many cases, an IAC will malfunction (usually due to age, wear) by responding very slowly.  This, together with a small vacuum leak, can easily cause the symtoms you describe.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000017||BOILERMAKER||10-08-1999||10:08 AM||||I'm going to change the IAC this weekend, along with other things.  I figured that if the IAC is the original, then it has 130k on it along with the rest of the car and probably is a little tired.  I also figure that if I keep changing things out (the smaller the amount of $$, the better) I will find the correct source of the problem.  So far I am just an EGR into it and that was needed.||||reg||1
